DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ccoommpprreessss reduces the size of each _f_i_l_e using the Lempel-Ziv
     (LZW) adaptive coding technique, and uunnccoommpprreessss reverses the
     process.  If no _f_i_l_e is named, ccoommpprreessss reads the standard
     input and writes the compressed result to the standard out-
     put.

     When _f_i_l_e arguments are given, ccoommpprreessss replaces each one
     with a compressed file of the same name plus a .ZZ suffix,
     and copies the original file's mode, owner, group, and ac-
     cess and modification times onto it; the original is then
     removed.  uunnccoommpprreessss does the reverse: it expects each _f_i_l_e
     to end in .ZZ, appending that suffix if it is missing, and
     writes the expanded data to a file with the .ZZ suffix
     stripped.

     ccoommpprreessss run under the name uunnccoommpprreessss behaves as though -dd
     had been given; run under the name zzccaatt, it behaves as
     though -dd and -cc had both been given.  uunnccoommpprreessss and zzccaatt
     are the same binary as ccoommpprreessss under different names.

     A file already ending in .ZZ is left alone when given to
     ccoommpprreessss for compression, with a message that it already has
     that suffix.  If compressing a file would not save space,
     the .ZZ file is removed and the original is left unchanged,
     unless -ff was given.

     -bb_m_a_x_b_i_t_s
          Limit the width of the variable-length codes written
          during compression to _m_a_x_b_i_t_s bits (9 to 12 by default;
          wider limits use more memory and can compress somewhat
          better).  This build supports code widths up to 16 bits
          for decompression, but a compression request wider than
          15 bits is reduced to 15.  This option affects only
          compression; uunnccoommpprreessss always uses whatever code width
          is recorded in the file's header.

     -cc   Write the compressed or decompressed data to the stan-
          dard output instead of to a .ZZ file, and leave the in-
